  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 153 PM CDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nAn upper shortwave trough is currently sliding across the Central \nPlains into the Middle Mississippi Valley with the main ridge axis \nover the Great Lakes area. A weak surface boundary remains draped \nacross central Kansas into southwest Kansas. Highs are closer to \nnormal in the low 90s in north central KS with south central and \nsoutheast Kansas in the 90s to low 100s. Late this afternoon into \nthe early evening, instability will increase along the boundary with \nsteep low-level lapse rates especially in south-central KS, likely \nresulting in scattered storms developing along the boundary. A few \nstrong to severe storms are possible with damaging wind gusts as the \nmain hazard as DCAPE values are generally up to 1500 J/kg. PWATs \ncontinue to remain above the 75th percentile for this time of year \nwith areas approaching 2\" or greater, leading to efficient rainfall \nwith any storm that develops. As we move into tonight and the \novernight hours, another weak shortwave trough is progged to slide \nacross the Central Plains, shifting better rain chances into central \nKS. This activity is likely to linger into Sunday morning.\n\nBy Sunday morning, the cold front should push south of our area into \nOklahoma bringing slightly cooler temperatures for Sunday afternoon \nas winds shift to northerly. Highs will range from the mid 80s in \ncentral KS to the low 90s in south central with some areas in \nsoutheast KS in the low 100s. There is some uncertainty with timing \nof the front for southeast KS, which may result in hot conditions \nlingering there as heat indices are currently forecast between 102-\n108. Given the uncertainty, did not extend a Heat Advisory to the \narea but trends should be monitored. Slight rain chances (under 20%) \nmay be possible Sunday night into Monday mainly in southern KS near \nthe KS/OK border, though uncertainty is high. Near normal \ntemperatures will continue into Monday with highs in the upper 80s \nto lower 90s across the area. \n\nAs we remain in zonal to northwesterly flow aloft as the upper ridge \nbuilds back into the Rockies and a deep upper trough digs over the \nGreat Lakes, a few weak shortwaves embedded in the flow may slide \nacross the region resulting in slight rain chances both Monday and \nTuesday night. Confidence is high that most of the region will \nremain dry with this activity. For Tuesday, warmer conditions will \nlikely return as above normal 850 mb temperatures are progged. This \nwill result in highs rising back into the upper 90s to lower 100s. \nThough this heat might be short-lived as a frontal boundary is \nprogged to move south through the area on Wednesday. There is some \nuncertainty with the timing of the boundary, but its likely to end \nup in southern Kansas near the KS/OK border by early Wednesday \nafternoon. If this solution happens, highs will range from the upper \n80s in central KS to the upper 90s in southern KS. Better rain \nchances are possible Wednesday night into Thursday as a stronger \nshortwave trough slides across the region, though there is \nuncertainty and trends will continue to be monitored. For Thursday \nand beyond, near normal temperatures for this time of year appear \nlikely with highs in the 90s and lows in the 60s to low 70s.",
